    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Anthropogenic mass bio-extinctions across many taxa characterize the modern era, including the well-documented decline of insect populations globally.  These declines have the potential to disrupt the role of insects in ecosystem processes, including insect herbivory, an important selective pressure for plants, which is also essential for regulation of plant community composition.  However, a lack of data on pre-insect decline herbivory, from at least 50 years ago, has prevented full understanding of the impacts of insect decline on this ecological process.  My research aims to assess whether herbarium specimens, by bearing evidence of insect damage on leaves, can be used to accurately capture historical insect herbivory.  Previous studies have both examined biases in herbarium collecting, as well as used herbarium specimens to measure herbivory, without knowing the precise accuracy.  \r\nIn this study, I: i) examine herbivory in live plants and paired pressed herbarium specimens, to determine if these specimens accurately captured the standing levels of herbivory and/or rates of herbivory; and ii)  assess long-term herbarium specimen data from California herbaria to assess possible trends in changes of herbivory over time.   I found that herbarium specimens did not accurately represent field herbivory, however, I detected a clear signal of decrease in herbivory over time in the California herbarium records.  These results clarify the usefulness of plant specimens in future studies using herbarium specimens and suggest that these cannot be used in future research to measure historical insect herbivory without correcting for inaccuracies.  However, herbivory is well recorded over time by herbaria.  In conclusion, herbarium specimens do not accurately reflect the real-life levels of insect herbivory measured in live plants. This highlights the importance of exploring potential mechanisms to correct for biases in herbarium collections. However, under the assumption that such limitations are consistent across time, herbarium specimens can be useful to conduct long-term comparative analyses of herbivory and assess the consequences of global insect collapse.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
